          %h2#HistoricalOrigins Historical origins
          %h3#Euclid Euclid: Monads as conceptual/arithmetic units
          %p
            On the historical origin of the notion in a mathematical sense, cf. eg.
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#GordonKusraevKutateladze02"} Gordon, Kusraev &amp; Kutateladze 2002, §2.2.7
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Kutateladze06"} Kutateladze 2006
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclid"} Euclid
            used “monad” (
            %em> μονάς
            , stem:
            = succeed "-)" do
              %em μοναδ
            %a{:href => "http://aleph0.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookVII/defVII1.html"} Book VII
            of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elements"} Elements
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            300 BC) in the following way:
          %blockquote
            %p#ElementsVIIDef1
              %strong> Book VII, Definition 1
              \:
            %p
              mονάς ἐστιν, καθ’ ἣν ἕκαστον τῶν ὄντων ἓν λέγεται. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Euc.+7.Def.1&fromdoc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085"}> source
              )
            %p
              A monad is that by virtue of which each of the things that exist is called one. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D1"}> source
              )
            %p#ElementsVIIDef2
              %strong> Book VII, Definition 2
              \:
            %p
              ἀριθμὸς δὲ τὸ ἐκ μονάδων συγκείμενον πλῆθος. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Euc.+7.def.2&fromdoc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085"}> source
              )
            %p
              A number is a multitude composed of monads. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D2"}> source
              )
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sextus+Empiricus"} Sextus Empiricus
            (2nd century AD) expands on this as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p A whole as such is indivisible and a monad, since it is a monad, is not divisible. Or, if it splits into many pieces it becomes a union of many monads rather than a &amp;lbrack;simple&amp;rbrack; monad.
          %p
            The term “monad” (
            %em> μονάς
            ) here is traditionally translated as “unit” without mentioning of the original terminology.
          %p
            More explicitly,
            %a{:href => "#Kutateladze06"} Kutateladze 2006
            (
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0608298.pdf#page=2"}> p. 2
            ) suggests to understand this as
            %em conceptual units for the purpose of counting
            – and thus to read the phrase “is called one” in
            %a{:href => "#ElementsVIIDef1"} Def. 1
            in the sense of counting, as in: “one sheep here, another one there” – which happens
            %em by virtue
            of an identification (what today we would call a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            with a
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
          %p
            This brings out the distinction between Euclid’s notion of monads and that of geometrical points from
            %a{:href => "http://aleph0.clarku.edu/~djoyce/elements/bookI/defI1.html"} Book I
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elements"} Elements
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong Book I, Definition 1
            %p
              Σημεῖόν ἐστιν, οὗ μέρος οὐθέν. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085%3Abook%3D1%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D1"}> source
              )
            %p
              A point is that which has no part. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D1%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D1"}> source
              )
          %p
            While sheep are not actually without parts, we do have a “monadic” notion of their
            %em conceptual
            indivisible unit by virtue of which we call any sheep “one sheep” such as to count them
            = succeed "." do
              %em one-by-one
          %p In summary:
          %center
            Where Euclid's points are atoms in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %br/
            so his monads are units in the sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic"} arithmetic
          %p
            This also becomes clear by the way in which the concept of monad is used further in Book VII of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclid"} Euclid
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elements"} Elements
          %blockquote
            %p#ElementsVIIDef7
              %strong> Book VII, Definition 7
              \:
            %p
              περισσὸς δὲ ὁ μὴ διαιρούμενος δίχα ἢ ὁ μονάδι διαφέρων ἀρτίου ἀριθμοῦ. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D7"}> source
              )
            %p
              An odd number is that which is not divisible into two equal parts, or that which differs by a monad from an even number. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D7"}> source
              )
            %p#ElementsVIIDef15
              %strong> Book VII, Definition 15
              \:
            %p
              ἀριθμὸς ἀριθμὸν πολλαπλασιάζειν λέγεται, ὅταν, ὅσαι εἰσὶν ἐν αὐτῷ μονάδες, τοσαυτάκις συντεθῇ ὁ πολλαπλασιαζόμενος, καὶ γένηταί τις. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D15"}> source
              )
            %p
              A number is said to multiply a number when that which is multiplied is added to itself as many times as there are monads in the other, and thus some number is produced. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D15"}> source
              )
          %p
            These definitions evidently refer by “monad” to the element “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1"}> 1
            ” among the natural numbers.
          %p
            Other definitions in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elements"} Elements
            VII also refer to monads as the
            %em
              multiplicative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %em lesser
            number where we today say they are “divided” by a smaller number:
          %blockquote
            %p#ElementsVIIDef5
              %strong> Book VII, Definition 5
              \:
            %p
              πολλαπλάσιος δὲ ὁ μείζων τοῦ ἐλάσσονος, ὅταν καταμετρῆται ὑπὸ τοῦ ἐλάσσονος. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D5"}> source
              )
            %p
              The greater number is a multiple of the less when it is measured by the less. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D15"}> source
              )
          %p
            With that understood, we see Euclid’s crystal clear definition of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+numbers"} prime numbers
          %blockquote
            %p#ElementsVIIDef11
              %strong> Book VII, Definition 11
              \:
            %p
              πρῶτος ἀριθμός ἐστιν ὁ μονάδι μόνῃ μετρούμενος. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D11"}> source
              )
            %p
              A prime number is that which is measured by a monad alone. (
              %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0086%3Abook%3D7%3Atype%3Ddef%3Anumber%3D11"}> source
              )
          %p
            The use of the term “monad” as in “unit” appears again in
            %em Introduction to Arithmetics
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicomachus+of+Gerasa"} Nicomachus of Gerasa
            (c. 60 – c. 120 AD). For example, in Book 1 Chapter 7 (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Balboa21"} Balboa and Balboa 21
          %blockquote
            %p Number Is Limited/Definite/Bounded Multitude or a Composition of Monads/Units or A Flow of Quantity composed out of Monads/Units;
            %p Αριθµος εστι ωρισµενον πληθος η συστηµα µοναδων η χυµα ποσσοτητος συγκειµενον εκ µοναδων;
            %p On the one hand, The Even is, That which can be Divided into Two Equal Parts without A Monad/Unit Intervening in The Middle; whereas on the other hand, The Odd is That which cannot be Cut/Divided into Two Equal Parts by the before-mentioned Intervention of The Monad/Unit.
            %p µεν αρτιον εστι, ο οιον διαιρεθηναι τε εις δυο ισα µη µοναδος παρεµπιπτουσης µεσον, δε περιττον το µη δυναµενον µερισ− εις δυο ισα −θηναι δια την προειρηµενην µεσιτειαν την µοναδος.
          %p
            Part I of the
            %a{:href => "#Dooge26"} D’Ooge 1926
            translation of
            %em Introduction to Arithmetics
            contains further discussion about the concept of the monad in other mathematical texts (e.g. Theon of Alexandria (p.37), Iamblichus (p.96, 127), and Boethius (p. 134)).
          %h3#metaphysics Middle ages: Monads as metaphysical units
          %p
            The primarily mathematical notion of “monad” by the ancients (
            %a{:href => "#Euclid"}> above
            ) later receives a religious/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gnosticism"} gnostic
            connotation, following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pythagorean"} Pythagorean
            and later neo-Platonic doctrines.
          %p#Monoimus
            Wikipedia claims that an Arab gnostic known as
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monoimus"} Monoimus
            (150-210 AD) “is known for coining the usage of the word
            %em Monad
            in a Gnostic context.”
          %p#BookOf24Philosophers
            Then there is the first line in the “
            %em> Book of 24 Philophers
            ” (~1200 AD) sometimes (such as by Aquinus below) attributed to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermes_Trismegistus"} Hermes Trismegistus
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em Deus est monas monadem gignens is se unum reflectens ardorem.
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;Latin original according to
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "#Vinzent12"} Vinzent 2012
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Litwa-BookOf24Philosophers.jpg", :width => "570px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Litwa19"} Litwa 2019
          %p
            This line is quoted by
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Aquinas"} Thomas Aquinas
            (13th century) in his
            %em Summa Theologica
            (see eg.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://www.ccel.org/ccel/aquinas/summa.FP_Q32_A1.html"} here
          %p A metaphysical line of thought on “monads” continues in the Early Modern period, as with
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giordano+Bruno"}> Giordano Bruno
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://www.digitale-sammlungen.de/de/view/bsb10205694?page=1"} De triplici minimo
              (1591)
          %p who asserts that:
          %blockquote
            %p
              the substance of things is the minimum, of which there are three principal varieties: the general metaphysical minimum or
              = succeed ";" do
                %em monad
              = succeed ";" do
                %em atom
              %em point
          %p This according to:
          %ul
            %li
              Edward P. Butler,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Transformation and Individuation in Giordano Bruno’s Monadology
              %strong 3
              2 (2015) 57-70 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://philpapers.org/versions/BUTTAI-2"}> philpapers
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://philarchive.org/archive/BUTTAI-2"} pdf
          %p#LeibnizMonads
            Later
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leibniz"} Leibniz
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monadology"} Monadology
            (1714) continues to insist on including “souls” and “god” among monads in a way that remains mysterious if not puzzling.
          %p
            Despite (or because of?) the esoteric if not irrational nature of Leibniz’ monadology it has become the most widely remembered usage of “monad”, while its rational origin with Euclid is nearly forgotten (for instance Wikipedia’s
            %a{:href => "#WikipediaMonadPhiliosophy"} entries
            do not mention Euclid).
          %p At the same time, both Leibniz and Bruno seem to have been following a tradition of thought that originated deeper in the middle ages:
          %blockquote
            %p the striking similarities between aspects of Leibniz’s monadology and Bruno’s doctrine of minims are probably attributable to the sources and philosophical interests that they shared in common
          %p This according to
          %ul
            %li
              Dilwyn Knox,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/Archives/spr2023/entries/bruno/"} Giordano Bruno
          %h2#modern_mathematical_notions Modern mathematical notions
          %p In the second half of the 20th century a couple of definitions in rigorous modern mathematics are introduced under the name of “monads”:
          %h3#MonadsOfInfinitesimals Monads of infinitesimals
          %p
            It certainly seems (but remains implicit) that
            %a{:href => "nonstandard+analysis#Robinson66"} Robinson 1966 (p. 57)
            meant to follow Euclid’s and/or Leibniz’s terminology when introducing the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+nonstandard+analysis"} monad in nonstandard analysis
          %p
            meaning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhoods"} infinitesimal neighbourhoods
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            (as such also used in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "infinitesimal+neighborhood#Kock80"} Kock 1980
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "nonstandard+analysis#Robinson66"} Robinson 1966
            refers to Leibniz extensively, but always with respect to Leibniz’s discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus"} calculus
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em Monadology
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of a point is more than that point, it does not contain any two distinct (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"}> global
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
            and in this sense is indivisible, whence the “monad”-terminology here seems to match well with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Euclid"} original notion by Euclid
          %h3#MonadsOnCategories Monads on categories
          %p
            Just a few years later,
            %a{:href => "monad#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967
            introduces the term
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical algebra
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Benabou-MonadDefinition.jpg", :width => "650px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "monad#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, p. 39
          %p
            Bénabou’s explicit reasoning on this choice of terminology has not survived, but (see the historical discussion
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "monad#Etymology"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+2-functors"} lax 2-functors
            of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \to Cat
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+points"} global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} elements
            or “units” in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theoretic sense
          %p
            Notice that
            %a{:href => "monad#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, p. 39
            follows the tradition of denoting a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            (here: the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            as (lax)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
            of “1” (in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Euclid"} above
          %p
            At the same time, with hindsight one may notice that the construction of infinitesimal monads in the
            %a{:href => "MonadsOfInfinitesimals"} above
            sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhoods"} infinitesimal neighbourhoods
            is (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "infinitesimal+disk+bundle#MonadicityAdjointToJetBundles"} there
          %h3#MonadsOfComputationalEffects Monads of computational effects
          %p
            The notion of monads in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+science"} computer science
            – as a model for “forms of computation” with computational
            %em effects
            within otherwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming+languages"} functional programming languages
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "monad+in+computer+science#Moggi89"}> Moggi 1989
            \&amp;rbrack; – is an equivalent perspective (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+triple"} Kleisli triple
            %a{:href => "#MonadsOnCategories"} above
            notion of monads on categories (the category in question now being the data
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+system"} type system
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} monad in computer science
            for more.
          %p
            Notice in this context that, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            an effect monad,
            = succeed "-effectful" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "monad+in+computer+science#BasicIdea"} there
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              \-effect handler
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "monad+in+computer+science#MonadModulesInIdeaSection"} there
          %h3#other Other
          %p Finally, there is the term
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson+monad"} Beilinson monad
          %p
            (maybe not widely used) which may be trying to evoke the picture not of an infinitesimal but a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"}> homological
            ” neighbourhood.
          %h2#conclusion Conclusion
          %p
            If we disregard the arguably (and often expressedly) non-rational (eg. gnostic) discussion of “monads” in the middle ages (understood very broadly, ranging from
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monoimus"} Monoimus
            in the 2nd century over hermetics in the 12th century AD to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leibniz"} Leibniz
            in the 17th century), then there is — contrary to what may be the common perception — a fairly coherent concept (probably partly intentionally, but partly by happy coincidence) connecting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclid"} Euclid
            (4th century BC) to the mathematics of the second half of the 20th century:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "nonstandard+analysis#Robinson66"} Robinson 1966 (p. 57)
            %a{:href => "monad#Bénabou67"} Bénabou 1967, p. 39
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "monad+in+computer+science#Moggi89"} Moggi 1989
          %h2#references References
          %p The ancient mathematical notion of monads as arithmetic units:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclid"}> Euclid
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elements"} Elements
                Book VII (~400-300 BC)
              %p translated by Thomas L. Heath (1956)
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Euc.+1.&fromdoc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0085"} Perseus Digital Library
          %p which appears again in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicomachus"} Nicomachus
                of Gerasa:
                %em Introduction to Arithmetic
                (~90-~120 AD)
            %li#Balboa21
              %p
                interlinear translation by Juan and Maria Balboa &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/nicomachus-arithmetic-balboa"}> archive
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Dooge26
